#: src/unstable-features.md:1
msgid "Unstable features"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:3
msgid ""
"Rustdoc is under active development, and like the Rust compiler, some "
"features are only available on nightly releases. Some of these features are "
"new and need some more testing before they're able to be released to the "
"world at large, and some of them are tied to features in the Rust compiler "
"that are unstable. Several features here require a matching `#!"
"[feature(...)]` attribute to enable, and thus are more fully documented in "
"the [Unstable Book](../unstable-book/index.html). Those sections will link "
"over there as necessary."
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:11
msgid "Nightly-gated functionality"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:13
msgid ""
"These features just require a nightly build to operate. Unlike the other "
"features on this page, these don't need to be \"turned on\" with a command-"
"line flag or a `#![feature(...)]` attribute in your crate. This can give "
"them some subtle fallback modes when used on a stable release, so be careful!"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:18
msgid "Error numbers for `compile-fail` doctests"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:20
msgid ""
"As detailed in [the chapter on documentation tests](write-documentation/"
"documentation-tests.html#attributes), you can add a `compile_fail` attribute "
"to a doctest to state that the test should fail to compile. However, on "
"nightly, you can optionally add an error number to state that a doctest "
"should emit a specific error number:"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:33
msgid ""
"This is used by the error index to ensure that the samples that correspond "
"to a given error number properly emit that error code. However, these error "
"codes aren't guaranteed to be the only thing that a piece of code emits from "
"version to version, so this is unlikely to be stabilized in the future."
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:38
msgid ""
"Attempting to use these error numbers on stable will result in the code "
"sample being interpreted as plain text."
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:41
msgid "`missing_doc_code_examples` lint"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:43
msgid ""
"This lint will emit a warning if an item doesn't have a code example in its "
"documentation. It can be enabled using:"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:50
msgid ""
"It is not emitted for items that cannot be instantiated/called such as "
"fields, variants, modules, associated trait/impl items, impl blocks, statics "
"and constants. It is also not emitted for foreign items, aliases, extern "
"crates and imports."
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:54
msgid "Extensions to the `#[doc]` attribute"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:56
msgid ""
"These features operate by extending the `#[doc]` attribute, and thus can be "
"caught by the compiler and enabled with a `#![feature(...)]` attribute in "
"your crate."
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:59
msgid "Adding your trait to the \"Notable traits\" dialog"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:61
msgid ""
"Tracking issue: [\\#45040](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/45040)"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:63
msgid ""
"Rustdoc keeps a list of a few traits that are believed to be \"fundamental\" "
"to types that implement them. These traits are intended to be the primary "
"interface for their implementers, and are often most of the API available to "
"be documented on their types. For this reason, Rustdoc will track when a "
"given type implements one of these traits and call special attention to it "
"when a function returns one of these types. This is the \"Notable traits\" "
"dialog, accessible as a circled `i` button next to the function, which, when "
"clicked, shows the dialog."
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:71
msgid ""
"In the standard library, some of the traits that are part of this list are "
"`Iterator`, `Future`, `io::Read`, and `io::Write`. However, rather than "
"being implemented as a hard-coded list, these traits have a special marker "
"attribute on them: `#[doc(notable_trait)]`. This means that you can apply "
"this attribute to your own trait to include it in the \"Notable traits\" "
"dialog in documentation."
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:77
msgid ""
"In addition to the \"Notable traits\" dialog, every type that implements a "
"`#[doc(notable_trait)]` trait renders a colored badge for that trait at the "
"top of its page, making the relationship easy to spot when browsing the type."
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:81
msgid ""
"The `#[doc(notable_trait)]` attribute currently requires the `#!"
"[feature(doc_notable_trait)]` feature gate. For more information, see [its "
"chapter in the Unstable Book](../unstable-book/language-features/doc-notable-"
"trait.html) and [its tracking issue](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/"
"issues/45040)."
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:88
msgid "Exclude certain dependencies from documentation"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:90 src/unstable-features.md:284
msgid ""
"Tracking issue: [\\#44027](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/44027)"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:92
msgid ""
"The standard library uses several dependencies which, in turn, use several "
"types and traits from the standard library. In addition, there are several "
"compiler-internal crates that are not considered to be part of the official "
"standard library, and thus would be a distraction to include in "
"documentation. It's not enough to exclude their crate documentation, since "
"information about trait implementations appears on the pages for both the "
"type and the trait, which can be in different crates!"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:99
msgid ""
"To prevent internal types from being included in documentation, the standard "
"library adds an attribute to their `extern crate` declarations: "
"`#[doc(masked)]`. This causes Rustdoc to \"mask out\" types from these "
"crates when building lists of trait implementations."
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:103
msgid ""
"The `#[doc(masked)]` attribute is intended to be used internally, and "
"requires the `#![feature(doc_masked)]` feature gate.  For more information, "
"see [its chapter in the Unstable Book](../unstable-book/language-features/"
"doc-masked.html) and [its tracking issue](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/"
"issues/44027)."
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:110
msgid "Document primitives"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:112
msgid "This is for Rust compiler internal use only."
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:114
msgid ""
"Since primitive types are defined in the compiler, there's no place to "
"attach documentation attributes. The `#[rustc_doc_primitive = \"...\"]` "
"attribute is used by the standard library to provide a way to generate "
"documentation for primitive types, and requires `#![feature(rustc_attrs)]` "
"to enable."
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:119
msgid "Document keywords"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:121 src/unstable-features.md:138
msgid "This is for internal use in the std library."
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:123
msgid ""
"Rust keywords are documented in the standard library (look for `match` for "
"example)."
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:125
msgid "To do so, the `#[doc(keyword = \"...\")]` attribute is used. Example:"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:130
msgid "/// Some documentation about the keyword.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:132
msgid "\"break\""
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:136
msgid "Document builtin attributes"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:140
msgid ""
"Rust builtin attributes are documented in the standard library (look for "
"`repr` for example)."
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:142
msgid "To do so, the `#[doc(attribute = \"...\")]` attribute is used. Example:"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:147
msgid "/// Some documentation about the attribute.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:149
msgid "\"repr\""
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:153
msgid "Use the Rust logo as the crate logo"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:155
msgid "This is for official Rust project use only."
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:157
msgid ""
"Internal Rustdoc pages like settings.html and scrape-examples-help.html show "
"the Rust logo. This logo is tracked as a static resource. The attribute `#!"
"[doc(rust_logo)]` makes this same built-in resource act as the main logo."
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:164
msgid "//! This crate has the Rust(tm) branding on it.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:168
msgid "Effects of other nightly features"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:170
msgid ""
"These nightly-only features are not primarily related to Rustdoc, but have "
"convenient effects on the documentation produced."
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:173
msgid "`fundamental` types"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:175
msgid ""
"Annotating a type with `#[fundamental]` primarily influences coherence rules "
"about generic types, i.e., they alter whether other crates can provide "
"implementations for that type. The unstable book [links to further "
"information](https://doc.rust-lang.org/unstable-book/language-features/"
"fundamental.html)."
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:181
msgid ""
"For documentation, this has an additional side effect: If a method is "
"implemented on `F<T>` (or `F<&T>`), where `F` is a fundamental type, then "
"the method is not only documented at the page about `F`, but also on the "
"page about `T`. In a sense, it makes the type transparent to Rustdoc. This "
"is especially convenient for types that work as annotated pointers, such as "
"`Pin<&mut T>`, as it ensures that methods only implemented through those "
"annotated pointers can still be found with the type they act on."
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:192
msgid ""
"If the `fundamental` feature's effect on coherence is not intended, such a "
"type can be marked as fundamental only for purposes of documentation by "
"introducing a custom feature and limiting the use of `fundamental` to when "
"documentation is built."
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:197
msgid "Unstable command-line arguments"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:199
msgid ""
"These features are enabled by passing a command-line flag to Rustdoc, but "
"the flags in question are themselves marked as unstable. To use any of these "
"options, pass `-Z unstable-options` as well as the flag in question to "
"Rustdoc on the command-line. To do this from Cargo, you can either use the "
"`RUSTDOCFLAGS` environment variable or the `cargo rustdoc` command."
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:204
msgid "`--write-doc-meta-dir`, and `--read-doc-meta-dir`"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:206
msgid ""
"These options control how rustdoc handles files that combine data from "
"multiple crates."
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:208
msgid ""
"By default, rustdoc will read the doc meta from the doc output dir itself "
"and merge them together. This is very easy to use in scripts that manually "
"invoke rustdoc, but it's also slow, because it performs O(crates) work on "
"every crate, meaning it performs O(crates<sup>2</sup>) work. When `--write-"
"doc-meta-dir` and/or `--read-doc-meta-dir` are supplied, this is turned off."
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:213
msgid ""
"When `--write-doc-meta-dir` is supplied, rustdoc will write the crate's "
"shared metadata to that directory. This is an _intermediate mode_ where it "
"may write some files to the doc output directory, but some features won't "
"work until it is finalized."
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:217
msgid ""
"When `--read-doc-meta-dir` is supplied, rustdoc runs in _finalize mode_. No "
"crate source code is passed to rustdoc when it runs in this mode. Multiple "
"`--read-doc-meta-dir` can be passed to rustdoc, so your build system can "
"split the state between multiple directories if that helps."
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:221
msgid ""
"`--write-doc-meta-dir` and `--read-doc-meta-dir` cannot both be passed to "
"the same rustdoc invocation."
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:223
msgid ""
"```console\n"
"$ rustdoc crate1.rs --out-dir=doc\n"
"$ cat doc/search.index/crateNames/*\n"
"rd_(\"fcrate1\")\n"
"$ rustdoc crate2.rs --out-dir=doc\n"
"$ cat doc/search.index/crateNames/*\n"
"rd_(\"fcrate1fcrate2\")\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:232
msgid ""
"To delay shared-data merging until the end of a build, so that you only have "
"to perform O(crates) work, use `--write-doc-meta-dir` on every crate, and "
"the last will use `--read-doc-meta-dir`."
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:235
msgid "You can use separate metadata directories:"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:237
msgid ""
"```console\n"
"$ rustdoc +nightly crate1.rs --write-doc-meta-dir=crate1.d -Zunstable-"
"options\n"
"$ cat doc/search.index/crateNames/*\n"
"cat: 'doc/search.index/crateNames/*': No such file or directory\n"
"$ rustdoc +nightly crate2.rs --write-doc-meta-dir=crate2.d -Zunstable-"
"options\n"
"$ cat doc/search.index/crateNames/*\n"
"cat: 'doc/search.index/crateNames/*': No such file or directory\n"
"$ rustdoc +nightly --read-doc-meta-dir=crate1.d --read-doc-meta-dir=crate2.d "
"-Zunstable-options\n"
"$ cat doc/search.index/crateNames/*\n"
"rd_(\"fcrate1fcrate2\")\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:249
msgid "Or you can use a single metadata directory for all of the crates:"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:251
msgid ""
"```console\n"
"$ rustdoc +nightly crate1.rs --write-doc-meta-dir=meta.d -Zunstable-options\n"
"$ cat doc/search.index/crateNames/*\n"
"cat: 'doc/search.index/crateNames/*': No such file or directory\n"
"$ rustdoc +nightly crate2.rs --write-doc-meta-dir=meta.d -Zunstable-options\n"
"$ cat doc/search.index/crateNames/*\n"
"cat: 'doc/search.index/crateNames/*': No such file or directory\n"
"$ rustdoc +nightly --read-doc-meta-dir=meta.d -Zunstable-options\n"
"$ cat doc/search.index/crateNames/*\n"
"rd_(\"fcrate1fcrate2\")\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:263
msgid "`--document-hidden-items`: Show items that are `#[doc(hidden)]`"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:264
msgid "<span id=\"document-hidden-items\"></span>"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:266
msgid ""
"By default, `rustdoc` does not document items that are annotated with "
"[`#[doc(hidden)]`](write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.html#hidden)."
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:269
msgid ""
"`--document-hidden-items` causes all items to be documented as if they did "
"not have `#[doc(hidden)]`, except that hidden items will be shown with a 👻 "
"icon."
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:271
msgid ""
"Here is a table that fully describes which items are documented with each "
"combination of `--document-hidden-items` and `--document-private-items`:"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:274
msgid "rustdoc flags"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:274
msgid "items that will be documented"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:276
msgid "neither flag"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:276
msgid "only public items that are not hidden"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:277
msgid "only `--document-hidden-items`"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:277
msgid "all public items"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:278
msgid "only `--document-private-items`"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:278
msgid "all items that are not hidden"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:279
msgid "both flags"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:279
msgid "all items"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:282
msgid ""
"`--markdown-before-content`: include rendered Markdown before the content"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:286 src/unstable-features.md:300
#: src/unstable-features.md:314 src/unstable-features.md:337
#: src/unstable-features.md:352 src/unstable-features.md:370
#: src/unstable-features.md:383 src/unstable-features.md:404
#: src/unstable-features.md:448 src/unstable-features.md:465
#: src/unstable-features.md:479 src/unstable-features.md:710
#: src/unstable-features.md:723
msgid "Using this flag looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:293
msgid ""
"Just like `--html-before-content`, this allows you to insert extra content "
"inside the `<body>` tag but before the other content `rustdoc` would "
"normally produce in the rendered documentation. However, instead of directly "
"inserting the file verbatim, `rustdoc` will pass the files through a "
"Markdown renderer before inserting the result into the file."
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:298
msgid "`--markdown-after-content`: include rendered Markdown after the content"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:307
msgid ""
"Just like `--html-after-content`, this allows you to insert extra content "
"before the `</body>` tag but after the other content `rustdoc` would "
"normally produce in the rendered documentation. However, instead of directly "
"inserting the file verbatim, `rustdoc` will pass the files through a "
"Markdown renderer before inserting the result into the file."
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:312
msgid "`--playground-url`: control the location of the playground"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:320
msgid ""
"When rendering a crate's docs, this flag gives the base URL of the Rust "
"Playground, to use for generating `Run` buttons. Unlike `--markdown-"
"playground-url`, this argument works for standalone Markdown files _and_ "
"Rust crates. This works the same way as adding `#![doc(html_playground_url = "
"\"url\")]` to your crate root, as mentioned in [the chapter about the "
"`#[doc]` attribute](write-documentation/the-doc-attribute."
"html#html_playground_url). Please be aware that the official Rust Playground "
"at https://play.rust-lang.org does not have every crate available, so if "
"your examples require your crate, make sure the playground you provide has "
"your crate available."
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:330
msgid ""
"If both `--playground-url` and `--markdown-playground-url` are present when "
"rendering a standalone Markdown file, the URL given to `--markdown-"
"playground-url` will take precedence. If both `--playground-url` and `#!"
"[doc(html_playground_url = \"url\")]` are present when rendering crate docs, "
"the attribute will take precedence."
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:335
msgid ""
"`--sort-modules-by-appearance`: control how items on module pages are sorted"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:343
msgid ""
"Ordinarily, when `rustdoc` prints items in module pages, it will sort them "
"alphabetically (taking some consideration for their stability, and names "
"that end in a number). Giving this flag to `rustdoc` will disable this "
"sorting and instead make it print the items in the order they appear in the "
"source."
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:348
msgid ""
"`--show-type-layout`: add a section to each type's docs describing its "
"memory layout"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:350
msgid ""
"Tracking issue: [\\#113248](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/113248)"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:358
msgid ""
"When this flag is passed, rustdoc will add a \"Layout\" section at the "
"bottom of each type's docs page that includes a summary of the type's memory "
"layout as computed by rustc. For example, rustdoc will show the size in "
"bytes that a value of that type will take in memory."
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:363
msgid ""
"Note that most layout information is **completely unstable** and may even "
"differ between compilations."
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:366
msgid "`--resource-suffix`: modifying the name of CSS/JavaScript in crate docs"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:368
msgid ""
"Tracking issue: [\\#54765](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/54765)"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:376
msgid ""
"When rendering docs, `rustdoc` creates several CSS and JavaScript files as "
"part of the output. Since all these files are linked from every page, "
"changing where they are can be cumbersome if you need to specially cache "
"them. This flag will rename all these files in the output to include the "
"suffix in the filename. For example, `light.css` would become `light-suf."
"css` with the above command."
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:381
msgid "`--extern-html-root-url`: control how rustdoc links to non-local crates"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:389
msgid ""
"Ordinarily, when rustdoc wants to link to a type from a different crate, it "
"looks in two places: docs that already exist in the output directory, or the "
"`#![doc(doc_html_root)]` set in the other crate. However, if you want to "
"link to docs that exist in neither of those places, you can use these flags "
"to control that behavior. When the `--extern-html-root-url` flag is given "
"with a name matching one of your dependencies, rustdoc use that URL for "
"those docs. Keep in mind that if those docs exist in the output directory, "
"those local docs will still override this flag."
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:396
msgid ""
"The names in this flag are first matched against the names given in the `--"
"extern name=` flags, which allows selecting between multiple crates with the "
"same name (e.g. multiple versions of the same crate). For transitive "
"dependencies that haven't been loaded via an `--extern` flag, matching falls "
"backs to using crate names only, without ability to distinguish between "
"multiple crates with the same name."
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:402
msgid "`-Z force-unstable-if-unmarked`"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:410
msgid ""
"This is an internal flag intended for the standard library and compiler that "
"applies an `#[unstable]` attribute to any dependent crate that doesn't have "
"another stability attribute. This allows `rustdoc` to be able to generate "
"documentation for the compiler crates and the standard library, as an "
"equivalent command-line argument is provided to `rustc` when building those "
"crates."
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:415
msgid "`--index-page`: provide a top-level landing page for docs"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:417
msgid ""
"This feature allows you to generate an index-page with a given markdown "
"file. A good example of it is the [rust documentation index](https://doc."
"rust-lang.org/nightly/index.html)."
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:420
msgid ""
"With this, you'll have a page which you can customize as much as you want at "
"the top of your crates."
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:422
msgid "Using `index-page` option enables `enable-index-page` option as well."
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:424
msgid "`--enable-index-page`: generate a default index page for docs"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:426
msgid ""
"This feature allows the generation of a default index-page which lists the "
"generated crates."
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:428
msgid "`--no-capture`: disable output capture for test"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:430
msgid ""
"When this flag is used with `--test`, the output (stdout and stderr) of your "
"tests won't be captured by rustdoc. Instead, the output will be directed to "
"your terminal, as if you had run the test executable manually. This is "
"especially useful for debugging your tests!"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:435
msgid "`--check`: only checks the documentation"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:437
msgid ""
"When this flag is supplied, rustdoc will type check and lint your code, but "
"will not generate any documentation or run your doctests."
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:440
msgid "Using this flag looks like:"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:446
msgid ""
"`--static-root-path`: control how static files are loaded in HTML output"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:451
msgid "'/cache/'"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:454
msgid ""
"This flag controls how rustdoc links to its static files on HTML pages. If "
"you're hosting a lot of crates' docs generated by the same version of "
"rustdoc, you can use this flag to cache rustdoc's CSS, JavaScript, and font "
"files in a single location, rather than duplicating it once per \"doc "
"root\" (grouping of crate docs generated into the same output directory, "
"like with `cargo doc`). Per-crate files like the search index will still "
"load from the documentation root, but anything that gets renamed with `--"
"resource-suffix` will load from the given path."
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:461
msgid "`--persist-doctests`: persist doctest executables after running"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:463
msgid ""
"Tracking issue: [\\#56925](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/56925)"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:471
msgid ""
"This flag allows you to keep doctest executables around after they're "
"compiled or run. Usually, rustdoc will immediately discard a compiled "
"doctest after it's been tested, but with this option, you can keep those "
"binaries around for farther testing."
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:475
msgid "`--show-coverage`: calculate the percentage of items with documentation"
msgstr ""

#: src/unstable-features.md:477
msgid ""
"Tracking issue: [\\#58154](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/58154)"
msgstr ""

